Distributed Locks

1. What is a Distributed Lock?
	A distributed lock ensures that only one node/process performs a particular operation at a time.
	Typical use cases:
		Updating shared data
		Running an expensive job once
		Calling an external API
		Preventing concurrent modifications
		
2. Two Reasons to Use a Lock
	A. Efficiency
		The lock only prevents duplicate work. if lock fails then two servers perform the same job. For this case, a simple single Redis instance may be enough.
	B. Correctness
		The lock is required to keep the system correct. if lock fails then two servers modify same resource.
		
3. The Big Problem with Lock Expiration
	suppose client A gets lock and process pauses then lock expires and client B gets lock. Now both will think they own lock and both can modify resources.
	The process could pause because of:
		Garbage collection
		CPU scheduling
		Page faults
		Disk/network delays
		OS pauses
		Long network delays

	The important point - You cannot safely assume that a process or network operation will finish within a particular time.
	
4. Why Checking the Lock Again Doesn't Fix It
	you may try to check lock and lock still valid so write data But the process can be paused between the check and the write. So check lock and it pauses then lock expires, another client gets lock and old one writes.
	
5. Solution: Fencing Tokens
	Every time a client gets a lock, it receives an increasing number. client A lock it gets 33 , client B lock it get 34
	The resource remembers the highest token it has accepted.
	So if Client A wakes up late: client A token = 33 then write storage sees latest token = 34 then rejects this request.
	
6. Why Redlock Has a Problem
	Redlock uses multiple Redis servers and majority voting.
	idea is 
		        Redis A
			Redis B
			Redis C
		Client ─ Redis D
			Redis E
	Acquire the lock on a majority of them.
	Redlock is not safe for correctness-critical locking because:
		It relies heavily on timing assumptions.
		Network delays can be unexpectedly large.
		Processes can pause unexpectedly.
		Clocks can jump or behave unexpectedly.
		It doesn't provide proper fencing tokens.
		
7. Timing Assumptions
	Redlock effectively assumes:
		Network delay       → small
		Process pause       → small
		Clock error         → small
		Lock TTL            → sufficiently large
	But distributed systems can experience delays.
	"Usually works" isn't enough when the lock protects correctness.
	
8. What Should You Use?
	If lock is only for efficiency
	A simple Redis lock can be sufficient.
	simple redis -> simple lock -> Occasional failure is acceptable.
	If lock is required for correctness use a system designed for strong coordination, such as:
		ZooKeeper
		A database with appropriate transactional guarantees
		Another proper consensus/coordination system
	important - Use fencing tokens when accessing the protected resource.
	
